{ % vim: set ft=djot : % }

# Djot language server

Build with `cargo build --release --bin djotls`, or install both tools
with `cargo install --path .`.
Run `djotls` to serve the Language Server Protocol over standard input
and output. The server writes only protocol messages to standard output.
The existing `djotfmt` command remains the formatter; `cargo run` still
selects it by default.

## Editor setup

Configure your editor'}s LSP client to run `djotls` for Djot buffers.
Use language ID `djot` and associate `.dj` and `.djot` files with it.
Both extensions work as open buffers. Automatic workspace indexing and
anchor lookup in unopened files currently recognize only the exact `.dj`
extension.

### Neovim 0.11 or newer

Install from the repository with `cargo install --path . --locked`.
Make sure `~/.cargo/bin` is on Neovim'}s `PATH`: `:echo
exepath('djotls')` should print the executable'}s path. If you only
built the project, replace `'djotls'` below with the absolute path to
`target/release/djotls`.

Put this in `init.lua`. It uses Neovim'}s built-in LSP client,
completion and snippets; `nvim-lspconfig`, Mason and a completion plugin
are optional.

``` lua
vim.filetype.add({ extension = { dj = 'djot', djot = 'djot' } })
vim.lsp.config('djot', {
  cmd = { 'djotls' },
  filetypes = { 'djot' },
  root_markers = { '.git' },
  workspace_required = false, -- Also attach to standalone files.
  on_attach = function(client, bufnr)
    vim.lsp.completion.enable(true, client.id, bufnr, { autotrigger = true })
    local function map(lhs, rhs, desc)
      vim.keymap.set('n', lhs, rhs, { buffer = bufnr, desc = desc })
    end
    map('gd', vim.lsp.buf.definition, 'Djot: go to definition')
    map('<leader>lr', vim.lsp.buf.references, 'Djot: references')
    map('<leader>ln', vim.lsp.buf.rename, 'Djot: rename symbol')
    map('<leader>ls', vim.lsp.buf.document_symbol, 'Djot: heading outline')
    map('<leader>lf', function()
      vim.lsp.buf.format({ bufnr = bufnr, name = 'djot', async = false })
    end, 'Djot: format')
    map('<leader>ld', vim.diagnostic.open_float, 'Djot: diagnostics')
  end,
})
vim.lsp.enable('djot')
```

Reopen a `.dj` or `.djot` file after loading the configuration. Check
`:set filetype?` (it should be `djot`) and `:checkhealth vim.lsp` if the
server does not attach. `:lua =vim.lsp.get_clients({ bufnr = 0 })` shows
clients attached to the current buffer.

In Insert mode, use `<C-x><C-o>` to request completion manually and
`<C-y>` to accept a selected candidate. Automatic triggering is enabled
above for the server'}s trigger characters. Try `[`, `[text][`, `[^`, or
`[text](#` with matching definitions in the document. If you already use
a completion plugin, keep its setup and omit `vim.lsp.completion.enable`
to avoid running two completion UIs.

`gd` on an inline link or a reference-definition destination opens the
local target file at its heading or explicit anchor. Without a fragment,
it goes to the start of the file. On a reference link'}s label, `gd`
first goes to the label definition in the current file; use it again on
that definition'}s destination to follow the link. The text part of a
reference link can also navigate directly to its destination.

External URLs remain available through `documentLink`. Neovim does not
provide a built-in `vim.lsp.buf.document_link()` action.
For an optional `<leader>lo` mapping, put the following function inside
`on_attach`, after the other `map` calls:

``` lua
map('<leader>lo', function()
  local params = vim.lsp.util.make_position_params(0, client.offset_encoding)
  local pos = params.position
  client:request('textDocument/documentLink', {
    textDocument = params.textDocument,
  }, function(err, links)
    if err then
      vim.notify(err.message, vim.log.levels.ERROR)
      return
    end
    for _, link in ipairs(links or {}) do
      local r = link.range
      local after_start = pos.line > r.start.line
        or (pos.line == r.start.line and pos.character >= r.start.character)
      local before_end = pos.line < r['end'].line
        or (pos.line == r['end'].line and pos.character < r['end'].character)
      if after_start and before_end and link.target then
        if link.target:match('^file:') then
          -- Open the file; cross-file heading fragments are not resolved.
          local uri = link.target:gsub('[?#].*$', '')
          vim.cmd.edit(vim.fn.fnameescape(vim.uri_to_fname(uri)))
        else
          vim.ui.open(link.target)
        end
        return
      end
    end
    vim.notify('No Djot link under cursor')
  end, bufnr)
end, 'Djot: open link')
```

The mapping opens local files in Neovim and delegates external URLs to
`vim.ui.open`. It opens cross-file links at the file level and ignores
query strings and heading fragments for local files. Use `gd` for
same-document and cross-file anchors.

Enable your client'}s completion UI and snippet support to use link
placeholders. Automatic completion triggering is a client setting;
manual completion also works. Ordinary bracket pairing remains an editor
setting. No custom protocol or editor-specific plugin is required by
this server.

## Supported features

The server supports:

- Whole-document formatting, using the existing formatter.

- A hierarchical heading outline (a flat outline for clients that do not
  support hierarchical document symbols).

- Workspace heading search through `workspace/symbol`, including
  unopened `.dj` files in the Git repositories containing the workspace
  folders. Names use `Title (path#anchor)`, with the path relative to
  the Git repository root and each heading'}s explicit or generated
  anchor. Standalone local files use their filename; non-file documents
  use their URI. No `containerName` is returned. Duplicate titles keep
  separate file locations. Search matches name substrings without case
  sensitivity, including paths and anchors.

- Definition navigation within the open document for link labels,
  footnotes, explicit IDs and implicit heading references.

- Workspace references for headings and explicit IDs through
  `textDocument/references`. Results include local references,
  cross-file inline links, images, reference-definition destinations and
  reference-link uses. Direct destinations select the fragment; indirect
  uses select the label. Query from a heading, ID, fragment or link
  text. Link labels and footnotes retain document scope; querying a
  reference link label finds uses of that label, while its text finds
  links to the destination anchor. `includeDeclaration` adds the
  selected definition once.

- Cross-file definition navigation for local inline links,
  reference-link text and reference-definition destinations. Relative
  paths resolve from the current document; query strings are ignored for
  file lookup and fragments locate explicit or generated anchors in
  `.dj` files. Target files need not be open or included in the
  workspace index. Links without a fragment (or with an empty fragment)
  navigate to the start of the file.

- Heading and explicit-ID rename through `textDocument/prepareRename`
  and `textDocument/rename`. Renaming a heading without an explicit ID
  updates its generated anchor and workspace links. If other generated
  anchors are renumbered, their links keep pointing to the same
  headings. Renaming a heading with an explicit ID preserves that ID and
  its fragment links; title-based references still follow the new title.
  Renaming an explicit ID updates its links without changing the heading
  text. A fragment or implicit title reference can also initiate the
  rename; generated fragments rename the owning heading, while explicit
  fragments rename the ID.

  Link paths, query strings and visible link text are preserved. For
  example, `[Old][]` becomes `[Old][New]` when its heading is renamed to
  `New`. Reference-link destinations are updated at the definition. New
  heading names are literal text; inline punctuation is escaped,
  replacing the old heading'}s inline formatting. Renames that would
  remove an embedded ID, create a duplicate anchor or retarget an
  implicit heading reference are rejected.

- Document-local reference-label and footnote rename, from a definition
  or resolved use. Only references bound to the selected definition
  change; duplicate definitions retain their existing precedence. Link
  text and destinations are preserved, including collapsed links:
  `[Old][]` becomes `[Old][New]`. Label whitespace is normalized and
  inline label punctuation is escaped as needed. Conflicting names,
  syntax changes and names that would capture other references are
  rejected. Link labels and footnotes have separate namespaces.

- File and folder moves through `workspace/willRenameFiles` and
  `workspace/didRenameFiles`. Incoming links follow moved targets, and
  relative outgoing links in moved `.dj` files keep their original
  targets. Batch moves resolve each link against the original locations.
  Internal links within a moved folder keep their spelling when they
  still resolve correctly. Images and reference-definition destinations
  are included; external URLs, query strings, fragments and link text
  are preserved.

  You can also invoke rename on the path in a local link destination,
  including an image or reference definition. The new name is a path
  relative to the document containing that link, or an absolute file
  URI. The server returns text edits at the original URIs followed by a
  `RenameFile` operation. This requires client support for
  `documentChanges` and the `rename` resource operation. The source must
  exist, the new parent directory must exist, and existing destinations
  are not overwritten. Heading and ID rename remain available on
  fragments.

- Completion for link labels after `[text][`, footnotes after `[^`, and
  anchors after `[text](#`.

- Workspace heading completion after `[text](`. Current-document
  headings sort before headings in other files. Completion inserts
  `#anchor` for the current file and a URI relative to the current
  document'}s directory for other files, including the explicit or
  generated heading anchor. Titles and destination prefixes can filter
  candidates. Existing closing parentheses are replaced, and destination
  paths and anchors are encoded as needed. Open target buffers
  contribute their unsaved headings. Percent-encoded same-document
  anchors resolve for definition navigation and reference diagnostics.

- Inline-link and reference-link templates after `[`. Accept a candidate
  to insert it; this does not automatically insert text without
  acceptance. Snippet-capable clients can move between the text and
  destination fields. Clients without snippet support receive plain
  text.

- Standard document links for direct links, reference links, images and
  reference-definition destinations. Relative paths resolve against the
  current document URI; Unicode and spaces are encoded as needed. Your
  client'}s open-link action can open the target even when it is not
  already open in the editor. External URLs are supported too.
  Cross-file query strings and fragments are preserved in document-link
  targets. Use definition navigation to jump to a local target'}s source
  position.

- Errors for unresolved reference labels, footnotes, local anchors,
  missing local target files and missing anchors in `.dj` targets.
  Duplicate definitions and IDs remain warnings. Read failures are
  reported separately as warnings, rather than as missing anchors. Other
  local file types are checked for existence; external URLs are not
  fetched or validated.

Completion suppresses suggestions in code, math and parsed attributes.
Reference completions replace an existing closing bracket and can remove
empty inline-link punctuation left by a link template. Destination
completion requests are triggered by `(`, `/` and `#`; clients may also
request completion manually. Destination completion lists are marked
incomplete so clients can requery while typing title or path prefixes.

Formatting defaults to 72 columns. Clients can pass a positive integer
`maxColumns` in formatting options to change it. The LSP indentation
option `tabSize` is not used as a line-width setting.

## Workspace scope and updates

Workspace folders are taken from the client, with `rootUri` (or legacy
`rootPath`) as a fallback. Each folder discovers its enclosing Git
repository, including linked worktrees. Git must be installed for
repository discovery. The index reads working-tree content for tracked
and non-ignored untracked files with the exact `.dj` extension.
Submodules and symbolic-link files are not traversed. Standalone open
documents remain supported without Git.

Open buffers take precedence over disk snapshots, including unsaved
changes. Closing a buffer restores disk content for searches,
completion, navigation and diagnostics. Clients with file-watching
support receive watcher registrations for `.dj` changes and file
creation/deletion. Workspace-folder and file-change notifications
refresh the index and open-document diagnostics. Opening, editing,
saving or closing a buffer also rechecks links in all open documents.
Without file watching, saving a buffer refreshes link diagnostics.
Searches, destination completion, references and cross-file navigation
refresh disk state and Git ignore rules on demand. Discovery and disk
analysis run on a blocking worker; unchanged source reuses its parsed
snapshot. Each refresh still enumerates and reads repository files.

Link resolution reads local `.dj` targets outside the index on demand,
including ignored files and paths outside the repository. Open-buffer
content takes precedence, including new files that have not yet been
saved. Diagnostics are published for open documents with their source
versions; results for superseded snapshots are discarded. Revalidation
currently checks all open documents. File reads and workspace link
validation run on blocking workers.

Rename searches indexed workspace documents and all open buffers,
including unsaved files. A linked target outside the index can be
renamed on demand; incoming links in other unindexed, unopened files are
not discovered. The server returns a workspace edit for the client to
apply and never writes files itself. Clients supporting
`documentChanges` receive versioned edits for open buffers; other
clients receive `changes`. If open buffers change during rename
planning, the request fails with `ContentModified` so the client can
retry.

File-move planning uses the same index and open-buffer snapshots. An
explicitly moved `.dj` file outside the index is read on demand to
update outgoing links; unindexed, unopened descendants of moved folders
are not traversed. Overlapping source moves and document destination
collisions are rejected. A `didRenameFiles` notification migrates open
buffers to the new URIs, clears old diagnostics, relocates moved
workspace folders and refreshes diagnostics. A buffer already opened at
the destination takes precedence over a migrated snapshot. Index
overlays treat equivalent encoded file URIs as the same file.

The client'}s file manager must send `willRenameFiles`, apply the
returned edits, perform the move and send `didRenameFiles` (when
advertised). A terminal `mv` or file-watch notification alone cannot
repair links automatically.

Workspace references use the same index and open-buffer snapshots as
rename. Linked targets outside the index are read on demand, including
their own local references; incoming links in other unindexed, unopened
files are not discovered. Results are deduplicated and sorted by URI and
source position. If open buffers change during the query, the client
receives `ContentModified` and can retry.

The server accepts incremental text changes but analyzes each document
version in full. Protocol positions use UTF-16. An incomplete delimiter
alone is not reported as a syntax error.

## Development

See the [contribution guide](../CONTRIBUTING.dj) for test prerequisites
and commands, and [memory measurement](memory.dj) for opt-in probes and
historical results. Snapshot and completion design notes live beside
`Document` and `completion_context` in [the analysis
module](../src/analysis/mod.rs); protocol concurrency notes live in [the
protocol adapter](../src/lsp.rs).
